ssardes","noun","genitive singular of Bussard",0]],"inflections":[["bussardes","bussard","genitive"]]},"butter bei die fische":{"entries":[["butter bei die fische",null,null,"Literally, “butter with the fish” (plural). Compare Dutch boter bij de vis (with a similar, but not entirely identical meaning).\nThe idiom is grammatically peculiar because it uses the accusative case (die Fische) with the preposition bei, which is incorrect by contemporary standard rules. The phrase is from a German Low German source. In many High and Low German dialects the word for by can express location (with dative) but also movement (with accusative). Compare other German prepositions varying between dative and accusative in the same way, e.g. in, auf, etc.",{"w":"Butter bei die Fische","g":"f"}]],"senses":[[367735,"butter bei die fische","noun","talking turkey, cutting to the chase (request for honesty, frankness, and straightforwardness)",0,"f",["informal","idiomatic","Germany"]],[367736,"butter bei die fische","noun","rolling up one's sleeves, getting down to brass tacks (request for honest effort, extra effort, all or nothing)",1,"f",["informal","idiomatic","Germany"]]]},"chlor":{"entries":[["chlor",null,"/kloːr/","From French chlore, derived from English chlorine, from Ancient Greek χλωρός (khlōrós, “pale green”) + -ine.",{"g":"n","decl":"|||s|-|-|-|-"}]],"senses":[[10943,"chlor","noun","chlorine",0,"n"]]},"detailverliebt":{"entries":[["detailverliebt",null,"/deˈtaɪ̯fɛɐ̯ˌliːpt/","Detail + verliebt"]],"senses":[[420398,"detailverliebt","adj","obsessed with detail",0]]},"dürften":{"entries":[["dürften",null,null,null]],"senses":[[102230,"dürften","verb","first/third-person plural subjunctive II of dürfen",0]],"inflections":[["dürften","dürfen","first-person,plural,subjunctive,subjunctive-ii"]]},"einwandfreieren":{"entries":[["einwandfreieren",null,null,null]],"senses":[[544757,"einwandfreieren","adj","inflection of einwandfrei:",0],[544758,"einwandfreieren","adj","strong genitive mas